---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/for/b2b-wholesale";
const title = "Shopify email tools for B2B wholesale: account and rep workflows";
const desc = "Choose a wholesale email stack by company-location context, approval access, rep coordination and reorder eligibility.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "What makes an email tool suitable for wholesale?",
    "answer": "It must preserve account and location context, coordinate approval and rep workflows, and send to the correct eligible contact. Demonstrate the integration using your own account model before choosing a vendor."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should approved accounts be onboarded?",
    "answer": "Confirm access first, then explain their catalog, minimum order rules, payment terms and rep contact. Stop first-order reminders when the qualifying order arrives."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can a wholesale tag prevent consumer email collisions?",
    "answer": "A tag alone is insufficient for buyers who also shop personally or purchase for multiple locations. Use order context, membership and subscription purpose, with explicit exclusions."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is Sequenzy the best choice for B2B?",
    "answer": "No universal ranking is established here. Evaluate its required integration, account model, operating controls and total cost against the same evidence requested from other candidates."
  },
  {
    "question": "Are the scenarios reported merchant results?",
    "answer": "No. They are proposed acceptance cases. No activation lift, revenue improvement, collision-free period or measured supplier advantage is claimed."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <nav><a class="link" href="/apps">Tool catalog</a> · <a class="link" href="/guides/selection-framework">Selection framework</a></nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl"><h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1><p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Wholesale email needs to know which account and location a buyer represents. Start with access, negotiated catalogs and rep ownership, then select the sender that can preserve that context. A bigger cart does not make a consumer welcome flow suitable for wholesale.</p></header>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Model the company, location and person separately</h2>
      <p>Shopify’s <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/b2b/companies-and-customers/creating-companies">company documentation</a> distinguishes a parent company, purchasing locations and their customer contacts. Catalogs and payment terms can vary by location, and a buyer with access to several locations chooses where to purchase. These relationships are the starting point for an email data contract.</p>
      <p>Proposed design: retain company ID, location ID, contact ID, recipient role, account state and catalog identifier. Identify the authoritative source and update path for each. An email profile with a wholesale tag alone cannot distinguish which branch placed an order or which catalog a purchasing contact can access.</p>
      <p>Ask a candidate vendor to demonstrate these fields arriving through your actual connector or API. Shopify company support does not establish that any email tool automatically syncs company metafields. If the destination is person-based, decide whether location context belongs on an event or in a separate account store; a single mutable profile field can overwrite the context of another branch.</p>
      <h2>Build approval onboarding around real access</h2>
      <p>A useful state sequence is application received, under review, approved with access, first order and repeat order. Make the approval system authoritative. An application acknowledgment should explain the review process; it should not promise purchasing access before access exists.</p>
      <p>Proposed acceptance case: a new restaurant account is approved for one location but its buyer cannot open the catalog. The first email should provide the correct access route and rep contact. Do not schedule reorder offers until access is resolved. A later first order should exit the first-order reminder sequence.</p>
      <p>Write onboarding around the assigned catalog, minimum order rules, case packs, payment terms and support route. Send sensitive negotiated prices only through an authorized account context. Prefer a secure catalog link when multiple recipients or forwarded messages would make a price table ambiguous. Choose reminder timing from the sales process rather than copying a fixed DTC welcome schedule.</p>
      <h2>Coordinate quotes, draft orders and rep follow-up</h2>
      <p>A quote, an order awaiting approval and a paid retail checkout are different states. Document which system owns each transition before enabling automation. A rep should be able to pause an automated follow-up during negotiation and see why the next message is due.</p>
      <p>Proposed acceptance case: a buyer requests a quote, changes quantities with a rep, then submits an order. Inspect queued follow-ups after each change. An obsolete quote reminder must not compete with the updated proposal or treat a draft as a completed reorder.</p>
      <p>Deduplicate by the business event and account context, not just by email address. Assign handling for delayed or repeated updates from CRM and commerce systems. Keep invoice and access messages with their operational owner; promotional opt-out must not silently remove necessary service communications, and a service contact must not automatically become a marketing subscriber.</p>
      <h2>Make replenishment location-specific</h2>
      <p>A restaurant chain may order different supplies at different branches. Build a reorder suggestion from that location’s purchased category and confirmed ordering pattern. Ask whether unusual orders were samples, seasonal stock or a one-time opening order before turning an interval into an automated reminder.</p>
      <p>Proposed acceptance case: one branch reorders while another has an unresolved delivery. A company-wide last-order field would hide the difference. Verify the intended recipient, location, current order state and rep hold before releasing either reminder. Do not imply a stock shortage or consumption rate that you have not measured.</p>
      <p>Catalog updates should explain the change that matters: case pack, minimum quantity, availability, replacement item or ordering access. Send only to contacts entitled to that catalog. A broad product newsletter can be useful, but it should not expose another account’s negotiated terms or consumer coupon.</p>
      <h2>Handle hybrid wholesale and consumer identities</h2>
      <p>A purchasing manager can also buy personally. Company membership alone is an unsafe reason to remove all consumer messages or apply wholesale prices to personal purchases. Record the order context and the purpose of each subscription.</p>
      <p>Proposed acceptance case: the same person places a personal order and a location order. Inspect both event payloads, links and resulting queues. The personal receipt should remain personal; the location reorder should reference the correct branch. Check a contact removed from a company as well, since stale membership can expose catalog information.</p>
      <p>Coordinate rep-managed campaigns and self-serve marketing through explicit exclusions. If account context is missing, route the record for review instead of defaulting to a retail discount. Keep an audit trail of approval and membership changes so the operator can explain why a recipient qualified.</p>
      <h2>Shortlist software by the integration work it removes</h2>
      <p>For a rep-led operation, ask a CRM-oriented candidate such as ActiveCampaign to demonstrate stage changes, ownership and pause controls using a real account model. For a hybrid ecommerce operation, ask a Shopify-oriented candidate such as Klaviyo to demonstrate location context and purchase exits. These are evaluation assignments, not claims of native support for every B2B field.</p>
      <p>If you already use Sequenzy, Brevo, Drip or another sender, evaluate its API and automation path against the same contract before replacing it. Ask who maintains custom mappings, what happens to failed updates and how an operator diagnoses a wrong recipient. No verified supplier ranking or price advantage is established by this guide.</p>
      <p>Request a quote using the eligible contacts, expected account messages, promotional sends, required service and integration maintenance. Include rep time and data repair. Do not select a platform from monthly orders or a revenue percentage; neither shows whether the tool can represent a multi-location buyer.</p>
      <h2>Measure the account outcome and preserve operating control</h2>
      <p>Define activation as a first qualifying order by an approved, enabled location within a stated observation window. Report the window and denominator, and separate access failures from nonresponsive buyers. A slow procurement cycle is not automatically an email failure.</p>
      <p>Track reorder behavior by location and category, rejected minimum-order attempts, support requests and rep-owned opportunities. Treat email-attributed revenue as attribution rather than proof of incremental revenue. If running a proposed holdout experiment, randomize at company level where contacts influence the same purchase, and document exclusions and success criteria before launch.</p>
      <p>Before production, retain representative event samples, expected recipients and expected queue transitions. Give sales and operations an escalation route for access mistakes, outdated terms and duplicate follow-ups. No merchant results or implementation tests are claimed here. Shopify’s account model was checked on 24 November 2026; connector coverage, plan eligibility, permissions and supplier pricing still require account-specific confirmation. See the <a class="link" href="/use-cases/segmentation">segmentation guide</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing-models">pricing models guide</a> for adjacent decisions.</p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
